What Car Shoppers Need to Know

Toyota is delaying the start of production for the all-new, all-electric 2027 Highlander SUV.

No new timing is yet announced for the ‘27 model, but Toyota confirms that the ‘26 Highlander will stay in production through December.

2026 Highlander sales are currently very strong, especially the hybrid variant.

Toyota has confirmed that production of the controversial all-new, electric-only 2027 Highlander SUV has been delayed. Originally expected to go on sale in late 2026, a Toyota spokesperson confirmed to Cars.com that the company is making “additional adjustments to the vehicle prior to launch.”

Related: 2027 Toyota Highlander: Toyota Reinvents Its Three-Row SUV as All-Electric Vehicle

The delay only affects the new 2027 Highlander, which is the bigger, all-electric version that Toyota showed us in February. The current 2026 Highlander, which is available in a gas or hybrid version, will continue to be made through December (and likely will be available on dealer lots for many months into calendar-year 2027).

Demand for the ‘26 Highlander is currently very strong, with hybrid sales especially high. Toyota maintains that the delay is to tweak the new all-electric model in some way, but it could just as easily be that the company is enjoying some extra profits on the fast-selling, top-dollar Highlander hybrids and would like to keep that cash machine ringing while RAV4 production continues to slowly ramp up.

